#. §2: `noKing`
#. §3: `hairyKing`
#. §4: `baldKing`
#: Game/Doc/Tactics.lean:49
msgid "§0 splits the goal into one goal for each way of building §1. For a situation of the\n"
"Sentences world, that is one goal each for §2, §3 and §4."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `decide`
#. §1: `SignType`
#. §2: `⊓`
#. §3: `SignType`
#. §4: `min`
#. §5: `⊓`
#: Game/Levels/Laws.lean:9
msgid "§0 works because §1 has three elements. A classical proposition is a set of possible\n"
"worlds, and nothing bounds how many worlds there are, so no truth table lists every proposition.\n"
"Facts about propositions are proved from laws instead.\n"
"\n"
"The laws here are those of a lattice, whose meet mathlib writes §2. Strong Kleene conjunction is\n"
"the meet of the order on §3, which Lean displays as §4 because that order is linear.\n"
"The intersection of two propositions is the meet of the order on sets of worlds, displayed as §5.\n"
"The same laws, with the same proofs, hold of both."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `inf_assoc a b c : a ⊓ b ⊓ c = a ⊓ (b ⊓ c)`
#. §1: `a ⊓ b ⊓ c`
#. §2: `(a ⊓ b) ⊓ c`
#. §3: `SignType`
#. §4: `min (min a b) c = min a (min b c)`
#: Game/Doc/Theorems.lean:10
msgid "§0, in every lattice, where §1 means\n"
"§2. On §3 this reads §4."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `inf_comm a b : a ⊓ b = b ⊓ a`
#. §1: `SignType`
#. §2: `a ⊓ b`
#. §3: `min a b`
#: Game/Doc/Theorems.lean:6
msgid "§0, in every lattice. On a linear order such as §1, Lean\n"
"displays §2 as §3."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `Account.Stronger.trans : a.Stronger b → b.Stronger c → a.Stronger c`
